  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1049 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nSevere thunderstorm chances return Tuesday afternoon as a low\npressure system pushes across the Mid Mississippi Valley. Ahead\nof thunderstorm activity, the pressure gradient will tighten\nacross the forecast area. The strongest gradient winds will exist\nwest of the Mississippi River over northeast Arkansas and the\nMissouri Bootheel. Sustained speeds of 25 mph with gusts nearing\n40 mph are forecast. As such, a Wind Advisory will go into effect\nat 8AM for the aforementioned areas.\n\nA typical cool season severe weather setup will be in place by\nTuesday afternoon, characterized by bulk shear of 40 kts and\nMUCAPE approaching 450 J/kg. The main environmental factors\nstorms will need to overcome for higher severe thresholds will be\nmeager lapse rates and persistent cloud cover. The most likely\nscenario for Tuesday afternoon and evening is a messy line of\nstorms that primarily poses a damaging wind threat for areas\nalong and west of the Mississippi River. Looping hodographs and\ndecent low-level turning indicate potential for brief, spin-up\ntornadoes embedded within the parent line of storms. As this\nactivity crosses the river, it will encounter less instability. A\nfew damaging wind gusts cannot be ruled out through the overnight\nhours, but the primary severe weather window will be through late\nevening. Thereafter, the threat transitions to more of a flooding\nconcern as precipitable water values approach the 99th\npercentile. Storm total rainfall remains between one and two\ninches.\n\nDry air will filter in behind Tuesday's system with temperatures\nremaining climatologically normal for early November. By the end\nof the week, a shortwave will eject out of the Rockies. This\nsystem will bring a cold front into the Mid-South on Friday.\nGiven the dry air in place, widespread rainfall is not\nanticipated with FROPA. Instead, expect cooler temperatures\nthrough the weekend as daily highs remain in the 60s with lows in\nthe 30s and 40s. There is potential for frost development both\nSunday and Monday mornings. However, since the Mid-South entered\nthe end of its growing season on November 1st, no frost headlines\nare anticipated.",
